# git blame skips the revisions listed in this file, so blame keeps naming
# whoever actually wrote a line instead of the commit that bulk-reformatted
# it. Without this file, one repo-wide "cargo fmt --all" run would bury the
# real authorship of every line it touches.
#
# To use this locally, run:
#   git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
# GitHub reads this file automatically, so blame views on github.com already
# skip these revisions without any setup.
#
# This file is deliberately empty of SHAs for now. The formatting change
# lands as a squash-merged pull request, so the commit that actually applies
# it on main does not exist until that merge happens, and its SHA cannot be
# known ahead of time. Do not add this branch's own commit SHA here: once
# the PR is squash-merged, that SHA is not an ancestor of main, and git
# blame fails outright ("fatal: cannot find revision ... to ignore") for
# anyone using this file on a fresh clone if an unreachable SHA is listed.
# Only revisions reachable from main may ever be listed here.
#
# Follow-up, once this PR is squash-merged: find the resulting commit on
# main, e.g.
#   git log --oneline -1 main
# and append it below, one SHA per line, with a short comment above each
# one saying what the commit was, for example:
#
# # cargo fmt --all (PRA-489)
# <squash-merge commit sha>
